{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792487402,"nonce":"qvnYSlB6jKYkPWn955CeoDUbVi8=","title":"release: kinds of release beyond images and a chart, and what still ties us to GitHub","message":"Raised in GB-17 (2026-10-19).\n\n## Kinds of release\n\n`[release]` knows one kind today: a web app (frontend, backend, admin images, maybe a Helm chart) deployed through GitOps overlays. Others we ship:\n\n- **Libraries**, published to a package registry: npm, PyPI, crates.io, …\n- **Versioned code or scripts**: the tag is the release, nothing is built.\n- **Binaries** (git-bug, git-board): built per platform and attached to the release.\n\nWhat GB-17 already covers:\n- A single-environment app cuts finals of what it runs and deploys nowhere: close to \"just push tags\".\n- `release cut --offline --no-deploy` keeps the tag local; the next online command pushes it, then builds and records it.\n\nOpen:\n- An artifact kind per `[release]` entry (image, chart, package, binary), each with a build step and a publish step.\n- Build while offline, publish once online, the same way the offline tag waits (today an offline cut defers the build as well as the push).\n\n## What still ties us to GitHub\n\nGo through each, and say where git-board stands and what is missing:\n\n1. Issues: git-bug, `refs/bugs`, with the bridge.\n2. Projects: the board.\n3. Releases: `refs/releases` and the tags (GB-17).\n4. CI/CD\n5. Code review (and Copilot review)\n6. Community: pull requests, merge requests, forks\n7. Rights and permissions\n8. Anything else?","files":null}]}